<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Green March</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1975">1975</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Green March was a mass demonstration organised by King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hassan-ii-of-morocco">Hassan II of Morocco</a> in November 1975, in which about 350,000 unarmed Moroccans crossed into Spanish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahara">Sahara</a> to press <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a>'s claim to the territory. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, weakened by Franco's final illness, avoided confrontation and signed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a> Accords on 14 November, handing the territory to Morocco and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mauritania">Mauritania</a>. The march began Moroccan control and a long war with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polisario-front">Polisario Front</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/green-march.jpg" alt="Green March" width="320" height="196" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Flaggingwill · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Green_March_1975.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Spanish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahara">Sahara</a> had been a Spanish possession since the last third of the 19th century. In 1958 the regime of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-franco">Francisco Franco</a> declared it the 53rd Spanish province, and in 1960 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> listed it as a non-self-governing territory. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a>, which claimed it as part of a Greater Morocco, faced a domestic crisis under Hassan II, who survived coup attempts in 1971 and 1972. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polisario-front">Polisario Front</a>, an anti-colonial <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> movement backed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a>, was founded in 1973. In 1974 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> agreed to prepare a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> for a UN-sponsored referendum.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a> sought an advisory opinion from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/international-court-of-justice">International Court of Justice</a>. Delivered on 16 October 1975, it found that some, but only some, Sahrawi tribes had ties of allegiance to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> of Morocco, but that no tie of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">territorial sovereignty</a> existed with either Morocco or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mauritania">Mauritania</a>, so self-determination still applied. Within hours Hassan II announced on television a peaceful march of 350,000 unarmed volunteers carrying Qur'ans and Moroccan flags to reunite the territory with Morocco.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>More than 524,000 Moroccans registered in three days, and quotas selected 350,000, among them 50,000 women. They were carried south in 113 special trains and thousands of trucks and buses to a tent city of 22,000 tents at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarfaya">Tarfaya</a>. From 31 October about 10,000 Moroccan troops also crossed the eastern frontier and clashed with Polisario forces. On 4 November the Spanish commander withdrew his troops 12 kilometres from the border behind minefields, with orders to avoid bloodshed while Franco lay dying.</p><p>On 6 November about 40,000 marchers cut the wire at Tah and advanced ten kilometres before halting at the Spanish minefields. The same day the UN Security Council adopted Resolution 380, which deplored the march and called for withdrawal. On 7 November a second column of 100,000 crossed from Abattekh. After talks at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agadir">Agadir</a> on 8 November, Hassan II announced on 9 November that the march had reached its objective, and the columns returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarfaya">Tarfaya</a> from 10 November.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>On 14 November 1975 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mauritania">Mauritania</a> signed the Madrid Accords. Spain gave up administration by 28 February 1976 but kept fishing rights and 35 per cent of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bou-craa">Bou Craa</a> phosphate mines. Morocco took the northern two thirds and Mauritania the south. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polisario-front">Polisario Front</a> proclaimed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahrawi-arab-democratic-republic">Sahrawi Arab Democratic Republic</a> on 27 February 1976, and the Western Sahara War followed. Mauritania renounced its claim in August 1979, and Morocco occupied its sector. A UN-brokered ceasefire took effect in 1991 with the MINURSO mission; the referendum has not been held.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a> holds that the march restored its territorial integrity on the basis of historical allegiance. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> has never recognised the Madrid Accords, and General Assembly resolutions describe the Moroccan presence as occupation.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Green March” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1975 Morocco took control of the former Spanish territories in the Sahara after the <b>Green March</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/francoist-spain">Francoist Spain · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>After Morocco's <b>Green March</b> in November 1975, the Madrid Accords handed administration to them, and Spain withdrew in early 1976.</blockquote><a href="/state/spanish-sahara">Spanish Sahara</a></li></ul></section>
